# Magic levels: \v \m \M \V, and which characters need a backslash at each.
# Substitutions wrap what matched in (…) so the match is visible in the buffer.

=== \v makes ( ) + special without backslashes
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  Use of "\v" means that after it, all ASCII characters except '0'-'9', 'a'-'z', 'A'-'Z' and '_' have special meaning: "very magic"
given:  |ababx
keys:   :s/\v(ab)+/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(abab)x

=== \v makes { a multi without a backslash
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  Use of "\v" means that after it, all ASCII characters except '0'-'9', 'a'-'z', 'A'-'Z' and '_' have special meaning: "very magic"
given:  |a12345
keys:   :s/\v\d{2}/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(12)(34)5

=== \v makes | alternation
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  |	   \|	    \|	     \|		nothing: separates alternatives
given:  |foo bar baz
keys:   :s/\vfoo|baz/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(foo) bar (baz)

=== \v makes = a multi
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  Use of "\v" means that after it, all ASCII characters except '0'-'9', 'a'-'z', 'A'-'Z' and '_' have special meaning: "very magic"
given:  |fo foo fooo
keys:   :s/\vfoo=/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(fo) (foo) (foo)o

=== \v makes < and > word boundaries
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  Use of "\v" means that after it, all ASCII characters except '0'-'9', 'a'-'z', 'A'-'Z' and '_' have special meaning: "very magic"
given:  |this is
keys:   :s/\v<is>/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |this (is)

=== \v needs a backslash for a literal dot
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  \.	   \.	    .	     .		literal dot
given:  |abc a.c
keys:   :s/\va\.c/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |abc (a.c)

=== \v needs a backslash for a literal brace
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  \{	   {	    {	     {		literal curly brace
given:  |a{2} aa
keys:   :s/\va\{/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(a{)2} aa

=== \v ( ) groups capture for \1
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  ()	   \(\)     \(\)     \(\)	group as an atom
given:  |ab
keys:   :s/\v(a)(b)/\2\1/<CR>
expect: |ba

=== in magic mode a brace is literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  \{	   {	    {	     {		literal curly brace
given:  |aa a{2}
keys:   :s/a{2}/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |aa (a{2})

=== in magic mode + is literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  Use of "\v" means that after it, all ASCII characters except '0'-'9', 'a'-'z', 'A'-'Z' and '_' have special meaning: "very magic"
given:  |aa+ a
keys:   :s/a+/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(a+) a

=== \m switches back to magic in the middle of a pattern
ref:    pattern.txt */\m*
quote:  Use of "\m" makes the pattern after it be interpreted as if 'magic' is set, ignoring the actual value of the 'magic' option.
given:  |aa+b
keys:   :s/\va+\m+/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(aa+)b

=== \M makes a dot literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\m*
quote:  Use of "\M" makes the pattern after it be interpreted as if 'nomagic' is used.
given:  |abc a.c
keys:   :s/\Ma.c/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |abc (a.c)

=== \M makes \. any character
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  .	   .	    \.	     \.		any character
given:  |abc
keys:   :s/\Ma\.c/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(abc)

=== \M makes a star literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  *	   *	    \*	     \*		any number of the previous atom
given:  |aa a*
keys:   :s/\Ma*/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |aa (a*)

=== \M still treats $ as end-of-line
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  $	   $	    $	     \$		end-of-line
given:  |ab ab
keys:   :s/\Mab$/(&)/<CR>
expect: |ab (ab)

=== \V makes everything but backslash literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  Use of "\V" means that after it, only a backslash and the terminating character (usually / or ?) have special meaning: "very nomagic"
given:  |abc a.c*
keys:   :s/\Va.c*/(&)/<CR>
expect: |abc (a.c*)

=== \V makes $ literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  $	   $	    $	     \$		end-of-line
given:  |a$b
keys:   :s/\V$/(&)/<CR>
expect: |a($)b

=== \V with \$ is end-of-line
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  $	   $	    $	     \$		end-of-line
given:  |ab ab
keys:   :s/\Vab\$/(&)/<CR>
expect: |ab (ab)

=== \V with \| still alternates
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  |	   \|	    \|	     \|		nothing: separates alternatives
given:  |a.b c*d
keys:   :s/\Va.b\|c*d/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(a.b) (c*d)

=== \\ is a literal backslash at every level
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  \\	   \\	    \\	     \\		literal backslash
given:  |a\b
keys:   :s/\v\\/(&)/<CR>
expect: |a(\)b

=== \M is immune to the pattern after it being magic-looking
ref:    pattern.txt */\v*
quote:  If you want to you can make a pattern immune to the 'magic' option being set or not by putting "\m" or "\M" at the start of the pattern.
given:  |a\[b\] ab
keys:   :s/\Ma[b]/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(a\[b\]) ab
